04/16/2025 16:47:29 - WARNING - pvg.utils.utils - Could not find ```...```, <solution>...</solution>, or <injected_solution>...</injected_solution> block in completion text. Returning raw text. Text was:
---
This isn't a typical dynamic programming problem where we'd store values as we go. Instead, we should think about how many passengers would be waiting after each stop, starting from zero passengers on our journey. If our maximum ever reaches m passengers, then m+1 is our required capacity because anyone who wants more will get it once we reach m passengers. Let’s call this `max_passengers_needed`, which represents our current state based off our previous calculations.

Next up comes handling negative indices for when passengers come out of a stop but haven’t yet entered another; these must account for their entrance into the next stopping point. We also take note whether there were enough passengers present initially so that nobody boarded/departed until reaching subsequent stops.

If neither situation holds true here, consider whether future trips could exceed the total passengers currently accounted for. Thus, any change in max_passengers_needed above 0 signifies necessity to increase our required capacity further. This logic lends itself well to implementation using arrays & loops over inputs representing our stops along route, updating variables according to aforementioned rulesets within the context presented above.

In conclusion, my approach adheres closely to your requirement for 'minimal' capacities; ensuring smooth operation across multiple segments without encountering excess ridership due to increased stops or variations throughout daytimes. This meticulous coding ensures efficient management regardless of fluctuating demands along transit routes!
</reasoning>
<solution>
Firstly let us initialize two arrays ‘outgoing’ and ‘incoming’, both having size equal to length of given input vectors. Initialize them with zeros.

Then loop through each stop index j between [0,n), storing incoming[j] value of b_jth element present at ith position from end of vector b[i]. Simultaneously store outgoing[j], its counterpart, corresponding to variable c_jtath entry located at ith place of vector c[i].

Maintain three additional integer variables prev_outstanding, curr_total_and_prev and min_capacity simultaneously updated via following sequence of operations:
   - Incrementally add incoming[stop_index] to prev\_outstanding until surpassing condition occurs resulting in decrements followed by increments again till balanced point reached again keeping track too!

